{
  "teamId": 158,
  "year": 2024,
  "narrative": "The 2024 Milwaukee Brewers were a team built on pitching, depth, and resilience — and they proved it by winning 93 games in a season that had its share of scares and heroics. They stumbled out of the gate in early April when Ryan Jeffers deposited a Bryse Wilson offering into the seats, but the offense soon found its footing. Gary Sánchez, Willy Adames, and Rhys Hoskins emerged as offensive pillars, delivering clutch home runs through April and May that kept the Brewers afloat and climbing in the standings.\n\nMay and June were the Brewers at their best — a blend of big moments from unexpected contributors like Jake Bauers, Jackson Chourio, and Andruw Monasterio who stepped up when the moment called. A Christian Yelich triple, a Bauers grand slam, and a Chourio grand slam in late June gave the team a powerful sense of identity. Even as the bullpen occasionally faltered — Joel Payamps surrendering a critical homer to Jake Cronenworth — the Brewers largely controlled games through a suffocating pitching staff and timely hitting.\n\nJuly brought turbulence. The Brewers went just 11-13 as the bullpen cracked under pressure, with Trevor Megill and Jakob Junis giving up devastating long balls at the worst moments. But the team refused to fold — Rhys Hoskins launched multiple clutch home runs and Willy Adames continued his power surge, and an extraordinary August (19-9) erased any doubt. Joey Ortiz, Jackson Chourio, and Adames fueled a torrid stretch that clinched Milwaukee's postseason berth and made them look like a legitimate contender heading into October.\n\nThe Wild Card series against the New York Mets became a microcosm of everything — guts, glory, and heartbreak. Jackson Chourio announced himself on the October stage with clutch hits and a home run, and Garrett Mitchell delivered a stunning blast to keep hope alive. But the Mets were unrelenting. Mark Vientos blistered Aaron Ashby in a critical moment, and in the decisive game, with the season hanging in the balance, Pete Alonso crushed a three-run home run off the Brewers' own closer Devin Williams — a gut-punch that ended Milwaukee's season just short of where they wanted to go.",
